  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\n- Scattered Showers, Isolated Thunderstorms Today\n\nScattered showers are developing within an area of 850-700mb warm\nair advection along with low level convergence from the 850mb low\nlevel jet. Instability is limited (100 to 500 J/kg) largely over \nLake Michigan and then along and south of a Ludington to Jackson \nline. An isolated thunderstorm is possible with the best chances\ntoward Lake Michigan as seen in recent lightning observations.\n\nThe best convergence and warm air advection will gradually shift\neast through this morning and early afternoon before most of the\narea clears out around 2pm. Some lingering showers and storms are\npossible toward central lower Michigan through the afternoon and\nearly evening.\n\n- Hot and Humid with Additional Chances for Thunderstorms\n\nHigh temperatures this week will be in the 80s to low 90s with the\nhottest temperatures on Tuesday and Wednesday. Dew points this\nweek will also be in the 60s to around 70. This will result in\nheat index values in the upper 80s to 90s. Some spots toward\nsouthern Michigan may see heat index values hit 100. Temperatures\ncool down for the rest of the week as northerly flow returns.\n\nWithin our hot, moist environment any little disturbance could\nspark off showers and thunderstorms through the first half of the\nweek. The low level jet and warm air advection will play a role\ntonight into Monday morning and Monday night into Tuesday. A warm\nfront will lift through the region Tuesday night into Wednesday\nmorning with a cold front moving through Wednesday evening. There\nis some uncertainty with how far south showers will extend until\nWednesday. Overall wouldn't be surprised if PoPs needed to be\ntrended higher for tonight into Monday morning and on Tuesday. \nMore ensemble members than not have precipitation, with the best\nchances Monday morning, Tuesday morning, and Tuesday night into\nWednesday morning.",
